    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">Can You Go Scuba Diving in Madeira, and How Do You Reach the Dive Sites?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>11 December 2026</span><span>6 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">Yes. Madeira has dive centres in Funchal, Caniço, Machico, Caniçal and Porto Santo, and the classic dives are the Garajau reserve east of Funchal, from the shore or a short boat ride, plus sunken warships off Cabo Girão and Porto Santo, reached by boat. The water stays between about 18 °C and 24 °C all year.</p>

      <h2>Where do the dive centres work from?</h2><p>Mostly from the south east of the island and from Porto Santo. Visit Madeira lists dive centres in Funchal, in Caniço next to the Garajau reserve, in Machico and Caniçal near the eastern tip, and on Porto Santo, the smaller island to the north east.</p><p>The centres split their dives two ways. Some sites are a walk into the sea from a beach or a rocky entry. Others are a boat ride away, either because the site is a wreck in open water or because the shore is all cliff. IFCN's fee table for the Garajau reserve covers both, dives from land and dives from boats, which tells you how common each one is.</p><h2>Which dive sites are worth knowing?</h2><p>Four names come up again and again, and they show the range of diving here, from a protected reef to deliberately sunk ships.</p><h3>The Garajau reserve</h3><p>Madeira's best known dive area lies just east of Funchal. Created in 1986, the Garajau Partial Nature Reserve covers 376 hectares along about 7 km of coast, from Ponta do Lazareto to Ponta da Oliveira, down to the 50 metre depth line. Fishing is banned, and its emblem is the dusky grouper, which near Garajau beach lets divers come remarkably close.</p><p>Visit Madeira names seven sites inside it: Lazareto, Pináculo, Mamas, Praia do Garajau, Baía dos Porcos, Arena and Galo. The rules are simple. A support boat must use one of the fixed mooring buoys rather than its own anchor, a dive flag must fly while divers are down, divers without a boat carry a signal buoy, and no fishing gear or spearguns may come in. Dives run by a company pay a small fee per diver to IFCN, the regional nature institute.</p><h3>The Afonso Cerqueira, off Cabo Girão</h3><p>A Portuguese Navy corvette 85 metres long, sunk on purpose off Cabo Girão on 4 September 2018. It lies at up to about 30 metres, is reached by boat, and is usually sold as a dive for advanced divers.</p><h3>The Bowbelle, at Madalena do Mar</h3><p>A wreck further west along the south coast, off the village of Madalena do Mar, listed by Visit Madeira among the island's dive sites.</p><h3>Porto Santo's two wrecks</h3><p>Off Porto Santo lie the Madeirense, a 70 metre cargo and passenger ship that crossed between the islands for decades and sank on 21 October 2000, now at 18 to 34 metres, and the corvette General Pereira d'Eça, sunk in 2016 at about 30 metres a short distance away. Both are boat dives. Getting there means the Lobo Marinho ferry from Funchal, about two and a half hours each way, or a short flight, so a wreck dive there fits best into a night or two on the island.</p><h2>Shore dive or boat dive: what is the difference?</h2><p>A shore dive is simpler and cheaper to organise. A boat dive reaches sites you cannot walk to. Here is how they compare in practice.</p><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Shore dive</th><th>Boat dive</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Typical sites</td><td>Garajau beach and nearby entries, Porto Santo's beach</td><td>Wrecks, offshore reefs, the far side of the reserve</td></tr><tr><td>Getting in</td><td>Walk in with your kit, often over stones</td><td>Roll or step in from the boat</td></tr><tr><td>Swell</td><td>Waves at the entry can stop the dive</td><td>Calmer start in open water, but the ride out can be bumpy</td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>Try dives, training, relaxed reef dives</td><td>Experienced divers, deeper sites</td></tr></tbody></table><p>On a pebble shore, the walk in is the hard part. Tanks are heavy, stones roll underfoot, and a small wave at the edge feels bigger with fins in your hand. If that worries you, ask the centre which entry it uses before you book.</p><h2>What is the water like?</h2><p>Mild and clear, but not tropical. Visit Madeira gives water temperatures of 18 to 24 °C through the year, with the coolest sea in late winter and the warmest from late summer into autumn. Its diving blog reports summer visibility often of 30 to 40 metres. A full wetsuit is the usual choice, and the cooler months call for a thicker one.</p><p>There is no coral reef here, despite the word reef in some site names. The bottom is volcanic rock, boulders and sand, and the life on it is Atlantic: groupers, rays, shoals of small fish and, now and then, visitors from the open ocean. Our article on <a href="/posts/are-there-coral-reefs-in-madeira">whether Madeira has coral reefs</a> explains why the water is too cool for them.</p><h2>Can beginners try it?</h2><p>Yes. PADI's try dive programme and its Junior Open Water course start at the age of 10, and first dives usually start in shallow water close to the entry. Expect a medical questionnaire before you go in, and be honest on it: ear, lung and heart problems matter underwater.</p><p>The wrecks are a different level. Depths of around 30 metres call for advanced training, so plan the Afonso Cerqueira or the Porto Santo wrecks for after you are certified, not as a first dive.</p><h2>Can you combine diving with a boat trip?</h2><p>Yes, but not on the same boat. Chifbay does not run dives or carry diving equipment. Our trips are about the coast above the water. On the <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip</a>, you pass the face of Cabo Girão, the stretch of sea where the Afonso Cerqueira now lies, before a swim stop at Fajã dos Padres, where you can jump in from the boat, and a second swim at Ribeira Brava.</p><p>A good pattern for a holiday is one dive day with a dive centre in Caniço or Funchal, and one day on a <a href="/private-boat-tour-madeira">private boat tour along Madeira's cliffs</a>. If you want the underwater side without tanks, the <a href="/posts/garajau-nature-reserve-madeira-guide">Garajau reserve guide</a> explains how to see the area from the shore.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is the best place to dive in Madeira?</h3><p>For most divers, the Garajau Partial Nature Reserve east of Funchal, a protected area since 1986 known for its large, calm dusky groupers.</p><h3>Is there a wreck to dive near Funchal?</h3><p>Yes. The corvette Afonso Cerqueira was sunk off Cabo Girão in 2018 and lies at up to about 30 metres. It is a boat dive for advanced divers.</p><h3>How cold is the water for diving in Madeira?</h3><p>The sea bottoms out near 18 °C and peaks near 24 °C, so a full wetsuit is the norm in every season.</p><h3>Do private boat trips in Madeira include diving?</h3><p>Chifbay's trips do not. They include swimming and paddle boards on the Day Trip. For a dive, book a dive centre.</p>

      <blockquote>Above Cabo Girão you look up at the cliff. Below it, a warship rests on the sand.</blockquote>
    </article>
    <p class="pref-src" data-pref-source><span>Enjoying our Madeira guides?</span> <a href="https://www.google.com/preferences/source?q=chifbay.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="#4285F4" d="M23.5 12.3c0-.8-.1-1.6-.2-2.3H12v4.5h6.5a5.6 5.6 0 0 1-2.4 3.6v3h3.9c2.2-2.1 3.5-5.1 3.5-8.8z"/><path fill="#34A853" d="M12 24c3.2 0 6-1.1 7.9-2.9l-3.9-3c-1.1.7-2.4 1.2-4 1.2-3.1 0-5.7-2.1-6.6-4.9h-4v3.1A12 12 0 0 0 12 24z"/><path fill="#FBBC05" d="M5.4 14.4a7.2 7.2 0 0 1 0-4.7V6.6h-4a12 12 0 0 0 0 10.8l4-3z"/><path fill="#EA4335" d="M12 4.8c1.7 0 3.3.6 4.6 1.8l3.4-3.4A12 12 0 0 0 1.4 6.6l4 3.1C6.3 6.9 8.9 4.8 12 4.8z"/></svg>Add Chifbay as a preferred source on Google</a></p>

